1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
The Pentagon.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's a global landmark, yet one of the world's

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
most mysterious buildings.

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Penetrate the walls of this fortress of American military

5
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
might and go only where insiders go to uncover its top secrets.

6
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
The Pentagon, next on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It has three times the office space of the Empire State

8
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,080
Building.

9
00:00:46,081 --> 00:00:51,320
It's twice the size of the Chicago Merchandise Mart.

10
00:00:51,321 --> 00:00:56,160
Five Capitol buildings can fit inside its walls.

11
00:00:56,161 --> 00:00:58,200
It's the Pentagon.

12
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:01,220
The name alone conjures up an imposing image

13
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:03,520
of American power and prestige.

14
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,880
As the largest office building in the world,

15
00:01:06,881 --> 00:01:08,660
it serves as the headquarters

16
00:01:08,661 --> 00:01:10,960
of the United States Armed Forces.

17
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:17,920
Every day, more than 24,000 men and women come here to work

18
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:20,920
on the vital and sometimes top secret business

19
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:23,020
of national defense.

20
00:01:23,021 --> 00:01:26,680
Beyond the Pentagon's massive size and awesome purpose

21
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:29,320
looms its impenetrable mystique.

22
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:33,820
It is a building that has transcended its original meaning,

23
00:01:33,821 --> 00:01:36,160
its original function to become a symbol.

24
00:01:36,161 --> 00:01:38,420
And as such, it is.

25
00:01:38,421 --> 00:01:41,800
It's still an extremely important part of what

26
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:46,440
is the American national identity.

27
00:01:46,441 --> 00:01:49,540
Despite possessing an image of invincibility,

28
00:01:49,541 --> 00:01:53,680
the Pentagon in reality has become quite vulnerable.

29
00:01:53,681 --> 00:01:56,740
It fails to meet current health and safety codes.

30
00:01:56,741 --> 00:02:00,480
Major building systems have deteriorated to such an extent

31
00:02:00,481 --> 00:02:02,780
that they pose a threat to national security.

32
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:09,340
For example, during Desert Storm,

33
00:02:09,341 --> 00:02:11,740
old water pipes burst when firefighters

34
00:02:11,741 --> 00:02:14,720
fought a blaze in the basement.

35
00:02:14,721 --> 00:02:18,780
7 million gallons of water flowed into that building,

36
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:21,820
and they almost put the Air Force Operations Center offline

37
00:02:21,821 --> 00:02:24,480
as the first aircraft were going in on Desert Storm.

38
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:27,820
That's a real impact to real people

39
00:02:27,821 --> 00:02:29,620
and the real world in our military.

40
00:02:29,621 --> 00:02:31,320
We can't allow that to happen.

41
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,780
To prevent such disasters from happening,

42
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:39,960
Congress established a fund in 1991

43
00:02:39,961 --> 00:02:43,100
for the Pentagon's first major renovation.

44
00:02:43,101 --> 00:02:46,060
The billion-dollar 15-year project

45
00:02:46,061 --> 00:02:48,280
calls for the complete gutting and remodeling

46
00:02:48,281 --> 00:02:52,080
of the building one wedge at a time.

47
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:54,140
The current renovations at the Pentagon

48
00:02:54,141 --> 00:02:58,340
will bring the building really into the 21st century.

49
00:02:58,341 --> 00:03:00,880
One of the fascinating things about the renovations

50
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:04,480
is they peel away these different levels and layers.

51
00:03:04,481 --> 00:03:07,540
In a certain sense, it's a piece of living history

52
00:03:07,541 --> 00:03:09,580
as they peel down these layers.

53
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,280
It's only fitting that the Pentagon's foundation

54
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:19,520
is rooted in war.

55
00:03:19,521 --> 00:03:23,520
As German forces conquered much of Europe in 1940,

56
00:03:23,521 --> 00:03:26,400
the War Department in Washington braced itself

57
00:03:26,401 --> 00:03:30,220
for America's inevitable entry into the conflict.

58
00:03:30,221 --> 00:03:34,220
The department's 24,000 civilian and military employees

59
00:03:34,221 --> 00:03:38,220
worked in 17 buildings, many of them temporary structures

60
00:03:38,221 --> 00:03:41,780
scattered throughout the District of Columbia.

61
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:43,980
The critical shortage of office space

62
00:03:43,981 --> 00:03:47,160
and the demand for more efficient military operations

63
00:03:47,161 --> 00:03:50,300
dictated the need to consolidate the department's expanding

64
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:51,660
workforce.

65
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:55,760
This urgent problem required a bold solution and a bold man

66
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,200
to execute it.

67
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:01,640
That man was Brigadier General Brayhan Somerville, the Army's

68
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:03,920
chief of construction.

69
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:07,880
Somerville originated the idea of one large building

70
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:11,620
to house the War Department people.

71
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:12,920
He drove it through.

72
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:15,560
He was very ambitious, very ruthless,

73
00:04:15,561 --> 00:04:17,660
very imperious man.

74
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:21,460
Although he would often rile his superiors,

75
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:23,460
they still supported him because they knew that he

76
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:27,000
would get things done.

77
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:31,900
On Thursday, July 17, 1941, Somerville called a meeting

78
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,820
of his staff and gave them their most challenging assignment.

79
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:39,320
On Monday morning, he expected basic designs for an office

80
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:42,860
building to be erected in nearby Arlington, Virginia,

81
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:45,560
where there was enough land to build an unprecedented

82
00:04:45,561 --> 00:04:46,720
structure.

83
00:04:46,721 --> 00:04:49,260
It had to be no taller than four stories,

84
00:04:49,261 --> 00:04:52,200
but large enough to house 40,000 people,

85
00:04:52,201 --> 00:04:55,360
with parking for 10,000 cars.

86
00:04:55,361 --> 00:04:57,600
After an intense working weekend,

87
00:04:57,601 --> 00:05:01,580
Somerville staff delivered their plans Monday morning.

88
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:04,800
Their design called for a 5 million square foot

89
00:05:04,801 --> 00:05:08,540
pentagonal building, consistent with its intended site,

90
00:05:08,541 --> 00:05:12,780
which was bounded on five sides by existing roads.

91
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:15,240
Credit for this unusual design goes

92
00:05:15,241 --> 00:05:19,440
to George Bergstrom, Somerville's chief consulting architect,

93
00:05:19,441 --> 00:05:23,380
whose Los Angeles firm designed the Hollywood Bowl.

94
00:05:23,381 --> 00:05:26,880
One reason Bergstrom chose a Pentagon for the War Department

95
00:05:26,881 --> 00:05:29,640
was that the shape made architectural reference

96
00:05:29,641 --> 00:05:33,000
to historical forts.

97
00:05:33,001 --> 00:05:36,400
But the big reason, chief reason, I believe,

98
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:41,840
was that it was by far the best in terms of internal movement,

99
00:05:41,841 --> 00:05:45,120
that it was much easier to get around in a building like that

100
00:05:45,121 --> 00:05:47,920
and in a rectangular building.

101
00:05:47,921 --> 00:05:49,820
Somerville received swift approval

102
00:05:49,821 --> 00:05:52,620
from his superior, General George Marshall,

103
00:05:52,621 --> 00:05:54,400
the Army Chief of Staff.

104
00:05:54,401 --> 00:05:56,740
But objections from several key sources,

105
00:05:56,741 --> 00:06:00,100
especially Congress, threatened to halt the project.

106
00:06:00,101 --> 00:06:04,900
They derided the proposed Pentagon as Somerville's folly

107
00:06:04,901 --> 00:06:07,980
and questioned its $35 million price tag

108
00:06:07,981 --> 00:06:10,280
and location outside the Capitol.

109
00:06:10,281 --> 00:06:13,340
Somerville's persuasive personality, however,

110
00:06:13,341 --> 00:06:16,400
prevailed, and Congress allocated the funds

111
00:06:16,401 --> 00:06:18,280
to build the Pentagon.

112
00:06:18,281 --> 00:06:20,980
The fight wasn't over yet.

113
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:23,280
A building of such scale and purpose

114
00:06:23,281 --> 00:06:26,480
demanded the attention of President Roosevelt.

115
00:06:26,481 --> 00:06:30,300
Franklin Delano Roosevelt is next to Thomas Jefferson,

116
00:06:30,301 --> 00:06:34,900
the American president most involved in architecture.

117
00:06:34,901 --> 00:06:37,240
Roosevelt had an aesthetic sense.

118
00:06:37,241 --> 00:06:39,140
He actually designed some buildings.

119
00:06:39,141 --> 00:06:42,340
He was very concerned about the look of Washington DC,

120
00:06:42,341 --> 00:06:43,980
where buildings be placed.

121
00:06:43,981 --> 00:06:46,680
And so when he was initially presented with this,

122
00:06:46,681 --> 00:06:48,180
he was a little reluctant.

123
00:06:48,181 --> 00:06:50,380
Really, what are they thinking about?

124
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:55,060
Fearing the prospective Pentagon

125
00:06:55,061 --> 00:06:56,560
would block the view of Washington

126
00:06:56,561 --> 00:06:58,720
from Arlington National Cemetery,

127
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:02,260
Roosevelt ordered its site moved less than a mile away

128
00:07:02,261 --> 00:07:06,260
to an area that included an abandoned airport and swampland

129
00:07:06,261 --> 00:07:09,860
in a rundown section called Hell's Bottom.

130
00:07:09,861 --> 00:07:12,700
Somerville disagreed with the president's choice,

131
00:07:12,701 --> 00:07:15,460
asserting that the cost of dredging and constructing

132
00:07:15,461 --> 00:07:19,620
pilings on the new site would add millions to the budget.

133
00:07:19,621 --> 00:07:22,840
It was a futile argument.

134
00:07:22,841 --> 00:07:25,460
Roosevelt had to remind him on one case

135
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:28,300
that he, Roosevelt, was the commander in chief,

136
00:07:28,301 --> 00:07:30,540
and that Somerville had to take orders

137
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:31,620
from the commander in chief.

138
00:07:31,621 --> 00:07:33,780
And that settled him then.

139
00:07:33,781 --> 00:07:35,920
The building's location changed,

140
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:37,820
but its design did not.

141
00:07:37,821 --> 00:07:41,200
Roosevelt approved its unique five-sided shape.

142
00:07:41,201 --> 00:07:44,580
With the president's support, building crews broke ground

143
00:07:44,581 --> 00:07:47,600
on September 11th, 1941.

144
00:07:47,601 --> 00:07:51,380
Less than two years later, a historic feat of conception,

145
00:07:51,381 --> 00:07:55,160
design, and construction would be miraculously achieved.

146
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:03,240
In September 1941, less than two months

147
00:08:03,241 --> 00:08:05,540
after designers had submitted their plans

148
00:08:05,541 --> 00:08:09,880
to General Somerville, construction began on the Pentagon.

149
00:08:09,881 --> 00:08:13,740
With both the war in Europe and demand for military office space

150
00:08:13,741 --> 00:08:18,480
escalating, Somerville set seemingly impossible goals.

151
00:08:18,481 --> 00:08:22,460
He wanted the Pentagon completed in just 14 months

152
00:08:22,461 --> 00:08:29,200
and a deadline of May 1, 1942, for initial occupancy.

153
00:08:29,201 --> 00:08:32,860
Construction of the Pentagon was a remarkable feat.

154
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:36,900
I can't think of any other building of comparable size

155
00:08:36,901 --> 00:08:40,300
that was built anywhere near this quick.

156
00:08:40,301 --> 00:08:44,440
It was, in many ways, an architectural engineering

157
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:46,640
marvel.

158
00:08:46,641 --> 00:08:49,480
Wartime austerity, cost concerns,

159
00:08:49,481 --> 00:08:52,520
and the belief that the building would become a storage facility

160
00:08:52,521 --> 00:08:56,380
after the conflict informed key construction decisions,

161
00:08:56,381 --> 00:08:58,660
including the choice of materials.

162
00:08:58,661 --> 00:09:01,820
To save steel for military use, the Pentagon

163
00:09:01,821 --> 00:09:06,660
was made out of reinforced concrete mixed from 680,000

164
00:09:06,661 --> 00:09:11,760
tons of sand and gravel dredged from the Potomac River.

165
00:09:11,761 --> 00:09:14,860
Reinforced concrete really cut down the amount of steel

166
00:09:14,861 --> 00:09:17,000
that you would need in the building.

167
00:09:17,001 --> 00:09:19,980
And the consequence is that you could build the thing that much

168
00:09:19,981 --> 00:09:23,100
faster and, in a certain degree, that much cheaper.

169
00:09:23,101 --> 00:09:25,940
There's raw concrete on the inside.

170
00:09:25,941 --> 00:09:27,420
Look in the light core.

171
00:09:27,421 --> 00:09:28,620
Look in different areas.

172
00:09:28,621 --> 00:09:30,220
And you'll see that this is a building that's

173
00:09:30,221 --> 00:09:34,160
to be done very, very fast.

174
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:36,320
The use of reinforced concrete

175
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:39,420
saved enough steel to build a battleship.

176
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:42,440
Alan Renshaw, the son of Clarence Renshaw,

177
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:44,540
the district engineer on the project,

178
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:50,040
recalls hearing how another key decision saved money and metal.

179
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:51,840
One of the stories that I always liked

180
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:54,340
that my dad used to tell, they were trying to figure out

181
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:57,640
how to put the stairways and the elevators.

182
00:09:57,641 --> 00:10:01,340
And at one point, General Marshall said, dadgummit.

183
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:03,740
He says, we're not going to have any elevators

184
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:05,040
in this building.

185
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:07,880
I don't want any fat generals running around the Pentagon.

186
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:10,480
And so that was the way it was.

187
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:11,680
There were no elevators.

188
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:13,080
There were no escalators.

189
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,000
Everything was with ramps and stairways.

190
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:21,240
The Pentagon's unadorned architectural style,

191
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:23,740
including its facade made of limestone

192
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:25,840
rather than more expensive marble,

193
00:10:25,841 --> 00:10:30,620
reflects most visibly the minimal approach to design and construction.

194
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:40,460
The style of the Pentagon might be called stripped classical, or WPA modern, or perhaps

195
00:10:40,461 --> 00:10:47,140
more humorously, Greco-deco, meaning that it was a type of architecture very common in

196
00:10:47,141 --> 00:10:53,560
public buildings in Washington and across the country in the 1930s, a rather austere

197
00:10:53,561 --> 00:10:56,660
classicism, where you still have the columns,

198
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:02,060
but none of the elaboration, none of the juicy details.

199
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:04,100
It's very simplified.

200
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:06,200
And it's entirely appropriate, of course,

201
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,800
to the type of enterprise that we're thinking about here,

202
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:13,120
the supreme command for the United States Armed Forces.

203
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:19,360
Though the architects intended their no-frills design

204
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:22,320
and frugal planning to expedite completion,

205
00:11:22,321 --> 00:11:25,160
The building of the Pentagon began slowly.

206
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:27,360
In the first three months of construction,

207
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:31,660
the 4,000-man crew worked at a pace that would require eight

208
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:33,260
years to finish the job.

209
00:11:39,480 --> 00:11:42,680
Once Pearl Harbor occurred, things changed entirely.

210
00:11:42,780 --> 00:11:45,180
And the pressures on the architects and the drafts

211
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:46,180
were enormous.

212
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:48,320
There were more than 400 of them at the peak

213
00:11:48,321 --> 00:11:52,860
engaged in the architectural drawings for the building.

214
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:57,800
They worked around the clock, as did the construction workers.

215
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:02,200
And at that kind of a pace, sometimes the actual construction

216
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:04,140
got a little bit ahead of the plans.

217
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,040
This sometimes resulted in some glitches in the building,

218
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:10,140
which persisted until the present time.

219
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:12,380
There are a few areas that have been discovered in the building

220
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:17,220
that was sort of sealed off and never used.

221
00:12:17,221 --> 00:12:21,160
At the peak of construction, the labor force more than tripled

222
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:25,420
to as many as 15,000 people working in three shifts,

223
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,560
24 hours a day, seven days a week.

224
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:33,740
As a young boy, an odd Alan Renshaw visited the hectic

225
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,380
site.

226
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:37,300
All sorts of people running around

227
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:39,080
looked like mass confusion to me.

228
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:41,520
So I went over and watched them pouring concrete down

229
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:44,740
a very large form.

230
00:12:44,741 --> 00:12:49,140
And this guy in a hard hat, probably as big as a house,

231
00:12:49,240 --> 00:12:50,480
said to me, he says, look out, kid,

232
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:51,980
don't get too close to that.

233
00:12:52,080 --> 00:12:52,780
I said, why?

234
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:56,180
And he said, well, we've lost three guys down these pits.

235
00:12:56,280 --> 00:12:58,380
So I later asked my dad about it.

236
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:00,280
And he said, oh, I think he's joshing your son.

237
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:02,200
He said, we haven't lost anybody in the construction

238
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:04,500
of the Pentagon yet.

239
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:06,860
Eventually, eight construction workers

240
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:10,740
would lose their lives on the Pentagon job.

241
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:13,640
Hank Neighbors, then a 17-year-old high school

242
00:13:13,641 --> 00:13:18,640
employed in the payroll department, witnessed one such fatality.

243
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:25,020
There was one very dramatic incident that I observed

244
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:29,580
that I sort of wish I hadn't, and that was a high-tension wire

245
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:32,920
slipped off somewhere up on the building and came down

246
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:36,760
and hit one of the workers right in the gut,

247
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:38,600
just cut him in half.

248
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:42,100
And of course, he died right on the spot.

249
00:13:42,101 --> 00:13:45,900
The men who gave their sweat, and in some cases their blood,

250
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,900
to build the Pentagon represented a cross-section

251
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:50,900
of the country.

252
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,840
Many of them had immigrated to America from Europe,

253
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:56,540
where their homelands were now at war.

254
00:13:56,640 --> 00:13:58,860
They worked side by side with laborers

255
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:01,860
from mainly southern states, most of whom

256
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:05,360
were happy to have their first steady job since the Depression,

257
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:10,000
even if it paid the unskilled among them only 30 cents an hour.

258
00:14:10,001 --> 00:14:17,000
Regardless of their backgrounds, these workers embodied the can-do spirit of the domestic war effort.

259
00:14:17,001 --> 00:14:23,000
American labor is going to build and build and produce and produce till we smash Japan.

260
00:14:23,001 --> 00:14:31,000
Spurred on by the attack on Pearl Harbor, construction workers completed the first wedge of the Pentagon

261
00:14:31,001 --> 00:14:33,906
less than eight months after groundbreaking.

262
00:14:33,907 --> 00:14:38,000
The first War Department employees moved in on April 29, 1942.

263
00:14:38,001 --> 00:14:43,000
two days ahead of Somerville's deadline for initial occupancy.

264
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:46,540
We were told that we were going to move into the Pentagon.

265
00:14:46,640 --> 00:14:50,540
And we arrived there, and I remember coming into the office.

266
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:54,040
I said, you know, it looks as if this is the first office

267
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:55,180
in the Pentagon.

268
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:57,180
And to this day, I think it was.

269
00:14:57,280 --> 00:14:59,660
One of the things that I remember most clearly

270
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:02,660
is having the opportunity on the lunch hour

271
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:05,620
to just roam around inside that vast building.

272
00:15:05,621 --> 00:15:08,380
that it was nothing but a shell of concrete.

273
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:10,560
There were no signs, no lines on the wall

274
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:12,060
to tell you where you're going.

275
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:17,560
And your first experience was, I'm going to get lost in here.

276
00:15:17,660 --> 00:15:22,680
For much of 1942, occupancy proceeded 24 hours a day

277
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:25,980
at a rate of about 1,000 employees per week.

278
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:28,080
While the Pentagon raced to completion,

279
00:15:28,180 --> 00:15:30,320
the building's sprawling infrastructure

280
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:34,320
transformed Arlington's suburban landscape.

281
00:15:34,321 --> 00:15:40,120
What is striking is that perhaps 40% of the cost

282
00:15:40,220 --> 00:15:43,060
did not go into the Pentagon building itself.

283
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:45,160
It went into the heating and cooling plant

284
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:46,460
and the road system.

285
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:48,360
The conception and realization

286
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:51,540
of the Pentagon's infrastructure is no less impressive

287
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:53,280
than the building itself.

288
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,200
For example, there was no precedent

289
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:58,720
for such a massive sewage plant, so engineers had to invent

290
00:15:58,820 --> 00:16:00,780
much of its equipment.

291
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:04,320
At the same time, road crews paved 30%

292
00:16:04,321 --> 00:16:07,620
of new access highways and erected 21 overpasses

293
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,120
and bridges, enough to rival many mid-sized cities

294
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:13,760
of the period.

295
00:16:13,860 --> 00:16:15,800
One of the great feats of the war

296
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:18,840
was the overall construction accomplished.

297
00:16:18,940 --> 00:16:24,780
It was also part of the big thinking, that is, bigger is

298
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:26,080
better.

299
00:16:26,180 --> 00:16:30,780
On January 15, 1943, the world's largest office building

300
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:33,780
was finished, officially named the Pentagon

301
00:16:33,781 --> 00:16:38,620
it cost $83 million and took an incredibly quick 16 months

302
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:42,420
to complete, just two months more than General Somerville

303
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:43,920
had anticipated.

304
00:16:44,020 --> 00:16:47,420
Workers needed the extra time to comply with a mid-construction

305
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:50,020
decision to add a fifth floor.

306
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:53,060
The speed with which the builders completed the job

307
00:16:53,160 --> 00:16:56,740
belies the quality of their work, a point appreciated

308
00:16:56,840 --> 00:17:00,140
by those currently charged with the Pentagon's maintenance

309
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:02,780
and renovation.

310
00:17:02,781 --> 00:17:05,780
What impresses me the most about the original construction

311
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:08,280
of the Pentagon is the vision and the foresight

312
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:09,780
that those people had.

313
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:12,280
When the building was first opened,

314
00:17:12,380 --> 00:17:15,520
the average individual in the building didn't even have a phone.

315
00:17:15,620 --> 00:17:17,020
There were no electrical outlets.

316
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:18,920
If you walk through that building right now,

317
00:17:19,020 --> 00:17:22,940
you find faxes, coffee makers, electric typewriters,

318
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:25,740
computers, communications equipment that was never

319
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:28,940
envisioned back in the 40s when this building was built.

320
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:31,680
And yet this building has been able to support all of those

321
00:17:31,681 --> 00:17:35,080
all of those increased activities without a major reconstruction,

322
00:17:35,180 --> 00:17:38,280
without a major renovation for 58 years.

323
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:39,780
So it's an amazing building.

324
00:17:39,880 --> 00:17:41,420
It stood the test of time.

325
00:17:41,520 --> 00:17:45,260
It's an emotional attachment that I have to the Pentagon.

326
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:48,160
You see how the building can respond to an emergency

327
00:17:48,260 --> 00:17:50,540
when it's handled properly and maintained properly.

328
00:17:50,640 --> 00:17:55,100
And you just develop a warmth for it that, you know,

329
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:57,480
it'll get you through, just like a sailor in a ship.

330
00:17:57,481 --> 00:18:03,240
In spite of the Pentagon's stature as an architectural achievement,

331
00:18:03,241 --> 00:18:09,320
the government held no formal dedication nor any other official observance marking its completion.

332
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:13,831
The pressures of wartime precluded any ceremony.

333
00:18:13,832 --> 00:18:17,080
Landmark status wouldn't be conferred upon the

334
00:18:17,081 --> 00:18:19,130
Pentagon for decades to come.

335
00:18:19,131 --> 00:18:24,220
But from the outset, it was clear that the building not only represented

336
00:18:24,221 --> 00:18:28,960
in architecture, but a major advance in urban planning.

337
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:32,960
A tour of the Pentagon's interior reveals a unique city

338
00:18:33,060 --> 00:18:33,960
within a city.

339
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:45,880
In 1943, the newly completed Pentagon

340
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:47,880
staggered the imagination.

341
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,880
There was nothing else quite like it anywhere.

342
00:18:50,980 --> 00:18:53,220
It was a building for which the phrase

343
00:18:53,221 --> 00:18:57,480
the phrase, size matters, seems to have been coined.

344
00:18:57,481 --> 00:19:00,360
The numbers told the story best.

345
00:19:00,361 --> 00:19:04,080
The building itself stood on 29 acres.

346
00:19:04,081 --> 00:19:08,200
The parking lots covered another 67.

347
00:19:08,201 --> 00:19:11,620
Inside, at 6 and 1⁄2 million square feet,

348
00:19:11,621 --> 00:19:15,400
ran 17 and 1⁄2 miles of corridor,

349
00:19:15,401 --> 00:19:18,000
not even the Empire State Building.

350
00:19:18,001 --> 00:19:19,960
Then the world's tallest building

351
00:19:19,961 --> 00:19:22,500
could compete with those figures.

352
00:19:22,501 --> 00:19:26,380
To the critics, however, bigger didn't mean better.

353
00:19:26,381 --> 00:19:28,960
Newsweek summed up the opinion of many

354
00:19:28,961 --> 00:19:30,300
when it dismissed the building

355
00:19:30,301 --> 00:19:33,540
as a simple penitentiary-like structure.

356
00:19:34,460 --> 00:19:37,540
But Architectural Forum found something visionary

357
00:19:37,541 --> 00:19:39,060
in the vastness.

358
00:19:39,061 --> 00:19:41,940
Perhaps this is the greatest lesson of the Pentagon,

359
00:19:41,941 --> 00:19:44,780
noted its 1943 critique.

360
00:19:44,781 --> 00:19:48,220
As building approaches the scale technically feasible,

361
00:19:48,221 --> 00:19:51,660
the distinction between architecture and city planning

362
00:19:51,661 --> 00:19:52,860
vanishes.

363
00:19:52,861 --> 00:19:58,020
The Pentagon gives a foretaste of the future.

364
00:19:58,021 --> 00:20:01,800
The size, the scale, this is a prototype

365
00:20:01,801 --> 00:20:04,820
for what would increasingly happen in post-World War II

366
00:20:04,821 --> 00:20:07,140
America of your mammoth structures,

367
00:20:07,141 --> 00:20:08,660
in a certain sense.

368
00:20:08,661 --> 00:20:10,360
And it's not too far-fetched.

369
00:20:10,361 --> 00:20:13,560
This is like the shopping malls that we have had,

370
00:20:13,561 --> 00:20:17,280
that this is close to designing for a city.

371
00:20:17,281 --> 00:20:19,980
but a city unlike any other.

372
00:20:19,981 --> 00:20:23,380
The Pentagon has no streets, roads, or avenues,

373
00:20:23,381 --> 00:20:28,400
but rather five floors, five rings, and 10 corridors.

374
00:20:28,401 --> 00:20:30,920
They're laid out so that it takes less than 10 minutes

375
00:20:30,921 --> 00:20:33,460
to walk between any two points.

376
00:20:33,461 --> 00:20:36,040
Clarence Renshaw, the district engineer

377
00:20:36,041 --> 00:20:38,400
who oversaw the building's construction,

378
00:20:38,401 --> 00:20:41,980
devised the numbering system to easily find any office

379
00:20:41,981 --> 00:20:44,180
in this concrete labyrinth.

380
00:20:44,181 --> 00:20:46,240
They were close to finishing the Pentagon,

381
00:20:46,241 --> 00:20:47,980
And General Marshall, he said, damn it.

382
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:50,600
He said, we've got to come up with a numbering system for this place.

383
00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:54,120
My dad says, well, General, I'll have one back to you in 30 minutes.

384
00:20:54,220 --> 00:20:54,920
He said, good.

385
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:59,420
My dad went to the room next door, and he got a piece of paper out,

386
00:20:59,460 --> 00:21:00,940
and he drew a little sketch of the Pentagon.

387
00:21:01,880 --> 00:21:04,200
In about five minutes, he came up with a scheme.

388
00:21:04,440 --> 00:21:05,600
Floor one, two, three, four.

389
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:08,320
Inner ring was A, outer ring was E,

390
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:11,820
and then each corridor around the spokes had a number.

391
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:15,120
So he brought that in to General Marshall about five minutes later.

392
00:21:15,121 --> 00:21:17,160
And he said, that looks good to me.

393
00:21:17,760 --> 00:21:19,240
My dad, he said, you know what?

394
00:21:19,241 --> 00:21:21,200
They haven't changed that numbering system yet.

395
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:25,580
Here's how to locate, for example,

396
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:30,140
Office 3D972 using Renshaw's numbering system.

397
00:21:30,700 --> 00:21:33,740
The first digit, 3, represents the floor number.

398
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:36,400
The letter D designates the ring.

399
00:21:37,160 --> 00:21:40,220
The next number, 9, identifies the corridor.

400
00:21:40,540 --> 00:21:43,620
And 72 refers to the office itself.

401
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:51,000
3D972 is where one finds Doc Cook, the longtime director

402
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:53,360
of administration and management.

403
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:57,500
Insiders refer to him as the mayor of the Pentagon.

404
00:21:57,600 --> 00:21:59,540
Well, you can regard the Pentagon

405
00:21:59,640 --> 00:22:02,840
almost as a city within a city, at least

406
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:05,780
within the county of Arlington.

407
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:08,880
And like a small city, it has many of the same problems,

408
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:12,680
provides many of the same municipal services

409
00:22:12,681 --> 00:22:14,680
as other small cities.

410
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:19,680
A sampling of the Pentagon services

411
00:22:19,681 --> 00:22:22,680
further illustrates the building's enormous size.

412
00:22:22,681 --> 00:22:26,680
On any given day, it's more than 20,000 civilian and military

413
00:22:26,681 --> 00:22:30,680
employees arrive by nearly every mode of transit,

414
00:22:30,681 --> 00:22:35,680
including the metro, which stops at the Pentagon's own station.

415
00:22:35,681 --> 00:22:40,680
Once at work, they make more than 200,000 calls from listings

416
00:22:40,681 --> 00:22:44,480
from listings found in the building's own 160-page phone

417
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:46,680
book.

418
00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:53,320
Consume 17,500 meals at various dining facilities.

419
00:22:53,420 --> 00:22:56,660
Patronize a post office that handles more than a million

420
00:22:56,760 --> 00:23:00,800
pieces of mail a month sent to or from the Pentagon's own zip

421
00:23:00,900 --> 00:23:06,880
code, and shop on a concourse the equal of any urban mall.

422
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:09,980
There are lots of services in the building to be had.

423
00:23:09,981 --> 00:23:12,480
And this had been necessary during the war,

424
00:23:12,580 --> 00:23:15,780
because people were working six days a week.

425
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:17,920
They were working 10 hours a day off,

426
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:19,720
and they didn't have time to get out in the shops.

427
00:23:19,820 --> 00:23:23,000
And shops were brought to them in the form of this very large

428
00:23:23,100 --> 00:23:25,460
area, and the building was set aside for that.

429
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:31,100
At the center of all this activity

430
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:34,540
is a five-acre courtyard, perhaps the Pentagon's most

431
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:37,340
unlikely feature, but the favorite one of those who

432
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:39,980
work there.

433
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:42,580
In the middle of this building, with 25,000 people

434
00:23:42,581 --> 00:23:44,760
who are working at a very frenetic pace,

435
00:23:44,761 --> 00:23:47,980
solving important problems and working important issues,

436
00:23:47,981 --> 00:23:50,020
you can walk out into that center courtyard,

437
00:23:50,021 --> 00:23:52,340
and it's like a transition into a different world.

438
00:23:52,341 --> 00:23:53,440
It's peaceful.

439
00:23:53,441 --> 00:23:54,440
It's quiet.

440
00:23:54,441 --> 00:23:56,360
And in the midst of these 25,000 people,

441
00:23:56,361 --> 00:23:58,860
you can have a few moments of solitude.

442
00:23:58,861 --> 00:24:01,600
It's a wonderful feature.

443
00:24:01,601 --> 00:24:05,820
In the middle of the courtyard stands the Ground Zero Cafe.

444
00:24:05,821 --> 00:24:08,380
According to Pentagon legend, the snack bar

445
00:24:08,381 --> 00:24:10,580
got its name during the Cold War,

446
00:24:10,581 --> 00:24:15,160
when Soviet satellite photographs spied much activity there.

447
00:24:15,161 --> 00:24:18,420
As a result, the communists aimed nuclear warheads

448
00:24:18,421 --> 00:24:21,940
at the snack bar, thinking it was a top-secret location.

449
00:24:21,941 --> 00:24:25,560
The only secrets at the cafe, joke Pentagon insiders,

450
00:24:25,561 --> 00:24:29,540
are the ingredients in the hot dogs.

451
00:24:29,541 --> 00:24:32,180
Such humor raises a serious question.

452
00:24:32,181 --> 00:24:35,400
How does the Pentagon, the Department of Defense,

453
00:24:35,401 --> 00:24:38,540
defend itself from enemy and terrorist attacks.

454
00:24:43,100 --> 00:24:45,580
We employ a lot of different kinds of security measures.

455
00:24:45,581 --> 00:24:48,080
We do some measures that are very, very visual.

456
00:24:48,081 --> 00:24:51,020
They're done purposely so, so people can see what we do.

457
00:24:51,021 --> 00:24:53,260
And there are obviously some measures that we do that are not

458
00:24:53,261 --> 00:24:54,960
so visible to the public.

459
00:24:54,961 --> 00:24:56,760
So we have to keep some things to ourselves.

460
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:03,720
Some of the things the Pentagon likes to keep to itself

461
00:25:03,721 --> 00:25:05,720
include the incinerator.

462
00:25:05,721 --> 00:25:08,880
Inside this facility, seen here for the first time

463
00:25:08,881 --> 00:25:11,780
on television, the Defense Protective Service

464
00:25:11,781 --> 00:25:15,460
shreds and burns two tons of classified documents a day.

465
00:25:21,560 --> 00:25:24,880
Pentagon personnel label each bag full of papers

466
00:25:24,881 --> 00:25:28,020
to prevent accidental reading and provide accountability

467
00:25:28,021 --> 00:25:29,580
should there be a security leak.

468
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:36,620
After these documents have been reduced to ashes,

469
00:25:36,621 --> 00:25:41,960
they're cooled off and carted away for disposal.

470
00:25:41,961 --> 00:25:44,460
At the Network Systems Management Center,

471
00:25:44,461 --> 00:25:48,760
officers monitor and resolve all computer-related problems.

472
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:52,040
On an average day, they prevent three hacking attempts

473
00:25:52,041 --> 00:25:55,540
and randomly filter 150,000 emails for threats.

474
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:03,180
And before any packages are delivered to the building,

475
00:26:03,181 --> 00:26:05,740
they must first be inspected at the latest addition

476
00:26:05,741 --> 00:26:09,560
to Pentagon security, the remote delivery facility.

477
00:26:09,561 --> 00:26:13,120
Designed to prevent an attack like the one at Oklahoma City,

478
00:26:13,121 --> 00:26:16,060
the RDF, as insiders call it, stands

479
00:26:16,061 --> 00:26:19,000
as a completely separate structure on the Pentagon

480
00:26:19,001 --> 00:26:20,560
grounds.

481
00:26:20,561 --> 00:26:23,860
That's a major step for us because we have over 200 trucks

482
00:26:23,861 --> 00:26:26,100
a day that come here to this facility.

483
00:26:26,101 --> 00:26:28,880
And they deliver everything from office supplies to mail.

484
00:26:28,881 --> 00:26:31,900
We have 80,000 pieces of mail come into the building a day.

485
00:26:31,901 --> 00:26:37,800
So those trucks coming in present opportunities for someone to do something that we would not want to occur.

486
00:26:37,801 --> 00:26:42,820
So this will allow us to have a proper screening facility before the materials then enter the building.

487
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:50,340
Perhaps the biggest threat to the Pentagon doesn't come from within or without, but rather from above.

488
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:58,180
The building's location in the flight path of Reagan National Airport makes it especially vulnerable to an airborne attack.

489
00:26:58,181 --> 00:27:02,420
The Ground Zero nickname for the cafe may not be such a joke

490
00:27:02,520 --> 00:27:03,920
after all.

491
00:27:04,020 --> 00:27:06,920
The challenge we have is how will we react to that?

492
00:27:07,020 --> 00:27:09,420
If something occurred, if someone tries to employ

493
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:13,420
chemicals or explosives, we have to have a plan on how we will

494
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:15,740
respond to that kind of event.

495
00:27:15,840 --> 00:27:18,740
We have a very detailed plan, but we're not going to advertise

496
00:27:18,840 --> 00:27:22,540
exactly what's all in the plan.

497
00:27:22,640 --> 00:27:25,840
What makes all these security measures necessary

498
00:27:25,841 --> 00:27:29,140
is that the Pentagon is the nerve center for the command

499
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:31,980
and control of national defense.

500
00:27:32,080 --> 00:27:35,280
A peak inside its highly classified areas

501
00:27:35,380 --> 00:27:38,380
promises to penetrate the mystique of the world's

502
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:39,980
greatest military power.

503
00:27:45,300 --> 00:27:47,800
The Pentagon's formidable image stems not only

504
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:50,600
from its size, scale, and shape, but also

505
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:53,840
from the serious business of national defense conducted

506
00:27:53,841 --> 00:27:55,640
inside its walls.

507
00:27:55,740 --> 00:27:57,440
Though the building's form and function

508
00:27:57,540 --> 00:28:01,040
have become synonymous, that linkage was never assumed

509
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:06,080
when the Pentagon was designed and built during World War II.

510
00:28:06,180 --> 00:28:07,680
There were many people who thought

511
00:28:07,780 --> 00:28:10,600
that after the war, there would be no need

512
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:12,600
for a building of this size.

513
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:15,100
The military would be shrunken down to size,

514
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:18,340
and the War Department could return to Washington,

515
00:28:18,440 --> 00:28:20,600
and this building could be used for other purposes,

516
00:28:20,601 --> 00:28:24,200
of storage, educational institution, archives,

517
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:26,540
all kinds of suggestions were made.

518
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:29,400
But of course, it just didn't come about

519
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:32,680
because the post-war period was a great deal different

520
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:35,020
than many people thought it was going to be.

521
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:37,120
And we found that we did have a need

522
00:28:37,220 --> 00:28:40,320
for a very large military establishment.

523
00:28:40,420 --> 00:28:43,360
The National Security Act of 1947

524
00:28:43,460 --> 00:28:46,260
officially established the Department of Defense

525
00:28:46,360 --> 00:28:48,620
and brought the Army, Navy, and Air Force

526
00:28:48,621 --> 00:28:52,380
Air Force under the Pentagon's considerable roof.

527
00:28:52,381 --> 00:28:55,420
The legislation created a bureaucracy as imposing as the

528
00:28:55,421 --> 00:28:58,540
building and gave rise to much of the Pentagon's humorous

529
00:28:58,541 --> 00:29:00,700
legend and lore.

530
00:29:00,701 --> 00:29:03,940
You've probably heard the old saw about the Western Union

531
00:29:03,941 --> 00:29:08,380
delivery boy that was in the Pentagon on a Friday afternoon.

532
00:29:08,381 --> 00:29:10,140
They closed the doors and he couldn't get out.

533
00:29:10,141 --> 00:29:12,280
By the time they found him the next week, he was already a

534
00:29:12,281 --> 00:29:13,580
brigadier general.

535
00:29:13,581 --> 00:29:18,320
Well, that cute story sort of illustrates some of the myths

536
00:29:18,321 --> 00:29:23,960
about the Pentagon, that it's terribly big, terribly confusing.

537
00:29:23,961 --> 00:29:26,000
Nicknames poking fun at the Pentagon

538
00:29:26,001 --> 00:29:30,460
as an overwhelming building and an unwieldy bureaucracy abound.

539
00:29:30,461 --> 00:29:33,980
Those who work there refer to it as Fort Fumble,

540
00:29:33,981 --> 00:29:38,860
the five-sided funny farm, and Disneyland East, among others.

541
00:29:38,861 --> 00:29:40,920
Well, I think the Puzzle Palace is the favorite one

542
00:29:40,921 --> 00:29:42,220
of most people.

543
00:29:42,221 --> 00:29:44,820
When I decided to write my book on the Pentagon

544
00:29:44,821 --> 00:29:47,660
a number of years ago, a number of people came up to me

545
00:29:47,661 --> 00:29:49,620
and says, you can't write a book about the Pentagon.

546
00:29:49,621 --> 00:29:50,500
And I said, why not?

547
00:29:50,501 --> 00:29:53,000
He said, nobody knows how this place works.

548
00:29:53,001 --> 00:29:54,500
I would agree with them, though.

549
00:29:54,501 --> 00:29:56,800
Nobody knows really how that place works,

550
00:29:56,801 --> 00:30:01,900
just because there's so many things going on in so many areas.

551
00:30:01,901 --> 00:30:04,900
Retired Air Force Major General Perry Smith,

552
00:30:04,901 --> 00:30:07,420
a former Department of Defense insider,

553
00:30:07,421 --> 00:30:11,120
authored Assignment Pentagon, the definitive handbook

554
00:30:11,121 --> 00:30:12,760
for new employees.

555
00:30:12,761 --> 00:30:14,500
It's kind of a frightening experience

556
00:30:14,501 --> 00:30:17,220
when you first go in there to realize that people are going

557
00:30:17,221 --> 00:30:20,460
to make some really important policies and decisions.

558
00:30:20,560 --> 00:30:22,620
And yet, even though you may have a lot of experience

559
00:30:22,720 --> 00:30:25,260
in the military and feel comfortable with your particular

560
00:30:25,360 --> 00:30:27,500
area of the military, you know that you're

561
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:29,700
going to be dealing with something very different,

562
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:33,200
very challenging, and in some cases, very disappointing.

563
00:30:36,040 --> 00:30:38,600
Many of the most important policies and decisions

564
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:42,380
at the Pentagon are made in the building's highest level areas.

565
00:30:42,480 --> 00:30:45,340
They include the office of the Secretary of Defense,

566
00:30:45,341 --> 00:30:48,100
One of the most impressive in Washington.

567
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:51,480
Here, the secretary, a civilian cabinet member,

568
00:30:51,580 --> 00:30:54,580
meets with his foreign counterparts and other dignitaries.

569
00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:01,380
Down the hall, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff

570
00:31:01,480 --> 00:31:04,600
holds court in an office befitting the highest ranking

571
00:31:04,700 --> 00:31:07,100
military officer.

572
00:31:07,200 --> 00:31:10,300
In the conference room, nicknamed the tank

573
00:31:10,400 --> 00:31:13,800
for its ship-like entry, the Joint Chiefs of Staff

574
00:31:13,801 --> 00:31:18,640
meet once a week to conduct their highly classified meetings.

575
00:31:18,740 --> 00:31:20,900
And when there's an international crisis,

576
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:24,540
the National Military Command Center, also known as the War

577
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:28,640
Room, becomes the Pentagon's nerve center and maybe the most

578
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:31,260
important room in the world.

579
00:31:31,360 --> 00:31:33,080
The National Military Command Center

580
00:31:33,180 --> 00:31:37,160
is manned 24 hours a day, 365 days a year,

581
00:31:37,260 --> 00:31:41,660
by a watch team of 15 people that

582
00:31:41,661 --> 00:31:46,000
that represent all of the services and a wide variety of expertise,

583
00:31:46,001 --> 00:31:50,760
and it's led by a general or admiral that's on watch.

584
00:31:50,761 --> 00:31:56,220
That general or admiral and his assistant are able to bring up whatever display

585
00:31:56,221 --> 00:32:00,160
they desire to watch at a particular time as events are unfolding

586
00:32:00,161 --> 00:32:04,900
on one of those screens. Now the beauty of the National Military Command Center

587
00:32:04,901 --> 00:32:09,260
is it can flex from those 15 people that are on watch

588
00:32:09,261 --> 00:32:15,160
24 hours a day to over 100 people if there is a crisis

589
00:32:15,260 --> 00:32:19,100
ongoing that needs to have that much attention.

590
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:22,140
Not all major policies and decisions at the Pentagon

591
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:25,080
are made in the building's upper echelon areas.

592
00:32:25,180 --> 00:32:28,020
Pentagon lore holds that staffers conduct some

593
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:31,480
of the most classified work in sub-basement bunkers

594
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:35,220
at the end of a complex web of underground tunnels.

595
00:32:35,320 --> 00:32:38,220
Not exactly, but close enough.

596
00:32:38,221 --> 00:32:43,420
I became aware when I was there that there were special rooms

597
00:32:43,520 --> 00:32:47,460
in the Pentagon, that not only was I not allowed to know

598
00:32:47,560 --> 00:32:51,860
where they were, I wasn't even allowed to go into them

599
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:54,060
or near them or talk about them.

600
00:32:54,160 --> 00:32:57,240
I think now I can say that, for example,

601
00:32:57,340 --> 00:32:59,800
one of the projects under me on my last tour

602
00:32:59,900 --> 00:33:03,820
was to work on the stealth aircraft.

603
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:06,580
And I had a limited access to that.

604
00:33:06,581 --> 00:33:11,660
And that office was in a very obscure part of the fifth floor

605
00:33:11,760 --> 00:33:14,860
where people just wouldn't ordinarily browse by.

606
00:33:14,960 --> 00:33:17,620
And it was pretty well disguised just

607
00:33:17,720 --> 00:33:19,120
to keep them out of the public eye.

608
00:33:22,220 --> 00:33:24,840
With all the highly classified military activity

609
00:33:24,940 --> 00:33:27,460
going on inside, outsiders often

610
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:30,840
view the Pentagon with suspicion and mistrust.

611
00:33:30,940 --> 00:33:34,680
As a result, the Pentagon is not only shrouded in myth,

612
00:33:34,681 --> 00:33:37,080
but misconception.

613
00:33:37,180 --> 00:33:39,220
One of the misconceptions about the Pentagon

614
00:33:39,320 --> 00:33:41,780
is that it is a temple of death.

615
00:33:41,880 --> 00:33:45,520
In fact, it's a very positive institution.

616
00:33:45,620 --> 00:33:48,320
It does a lot of good things.

617
00:33:48,420 --> 00:33:51,160
A great deal of civic action, not only in this country,

618
00:33:51,260 --> 00:33:53,200
but around the world.

619
00:33:53,300 --> 00:33:57,240
The Pentagon has carried all kinds of rescue and aid

620
00:33:57,340 --> 00:33:58,780
missions.

621
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:01,040
In spite of the civic actions the Pentagon

622
00:34:01,140 --> 00:34:03,280
has undertaken, the perception of it

623
00:34:03,281 --> 00:34:06,720
of it as a so-called temple of death was at its worst

624
00:34:06,820 --> 00:34:08,680
during the Vietnam War.

625
00:34:08,780 --> 00:34:11,760
Thousands of protesters opposing the conflict

626
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:15,660
demonstrated at the Pentagon, affecting both its policy

627
00:34:15,760 --> 00:34:17,560
and the people who worked there.

628
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:20,560
I was here for a period of about four or five months.

629
00:34:20,660 --> 00:34:23,800
And a number of protests occurred at that time,

630
00:34:23,900 --> 00:34:25,940
which was rather surprising to me.

631
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:29,640
And that's when I really became aware of the depth of feelings

632
00:34:29,740 --> 00:34:31,500
in the country about the war.

633
00:34:31,501 --> 00:34:35,100
At that time, they also asked us to start wearing civilian clothes

634
00:34:35,101 --> 00:34:38,660
to minimize the visible presence of the military,

635
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:42,840
not only in the Pentagon, but in most buildings in Washington.

636
00:34:44,080 --> 00:34:45,520
And we didn't feel good about that.

637
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:49,500
In a move designed in part to humanize the Pentagon

638
00:34:49,501 --> 00:34:51,900
and boost the morale of its employees,

639
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:55,740
the building began transforming many of its barren hallways

640
00:34:55,741 --> 00:34:58,780
into dedicated corridors in the 1970s.

641
00:34:58,781 --> 00:35:02,620
More than 20 such hallways commemorate illustrious events

642
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:08,220
in U.S. military history, legendary leaders,

643
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:13,020
and the diversity and sacrifice of those who serve their country.

644
00:35:13,120 --> 00:35:16,940
The most popular of these corridors is the Hall of Heroes,

645
00:35:17,040 --> 00:35:21,440
honoring the nation's more than 3,400 Medal of Honor winners.

646
00:35:21,540 --> 00:35:24,140
If there is a sacred spot in the Pentagon,

647
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:26,940
the Hall of Heroes is it.

648
00:35:26,941 --> 00:35:29,480
My favorite architectural feature of the Pentagon

649
00:35:29,580 --> 00:35:32,080
is the Hall of Heroes.

650
00:35:32,180 --> 00:35:34,580
You go there sometimes when you have a quiet moment,

651
00:35:34,680 --> 00:35:38,080
just walk in there and appreciate the heroism

652
00:35:38,180 --> 00:35:41,080
of the people on the wall.

653
00:35:41,180 --> 00:35:43,260
In spite of their decorative appeal,

654
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:45,700
these dedicated corridors couldn't conceal

655
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:48,400
the deterioration that threatened the Pentagon's

656
00:35:48,500 --> 00:35:50,300
vital operations.

657
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:52,400
To make the necessary improvements,

658
00:35:52,500 --> 00:35:55,700
the building would have to undergo a complete renovation

659
00:35:55,701 --> 00:35:59,700
that would rival its original construction in scope and scale.

660
00:36:05,700 --> 00:36:09,700
In 1992, the National Park Service declared the Pentagon

661
00:36:09,701 --> 00:36:12,700
a National Historic Landmark.

662
00:36:12,701 --> 00:36:15,700
The prestigious designation cited five unique features

663
00:36:15,701 --> 00:36:20,700
of the building, including its outer wall structure.

664
00:36:20,701 --> 00:36:23,700
Center courtyard,

665
00:36:23,701 --> 00:36:28,300
Terrace entrances and its five-sided shape,

666
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:32,400
many considered the special status long overdue.

667
00:36:32,500 --> 00:36:35,440
The Pentagon is very definitely an expression

668
00:36:35,540 --> 00:36:36,940
of its time and place.

669
00:36:37,040 --> 00:36:39,380
There is the austerity of the style.

670
00:36:39,480 --> 00:36:44,560
But it's also an expression of a particular patriotic quality.

671
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:47,560
And if you think of American public buildings of the last,

672
00:36:47,660 --> 00:36:51,000
say, 30 years, what distinguishes them

673
00:36:51,001 --> 00:36:54,800
distinguishes them from an ordinary office building.

674
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:56,340
Maybe there's an extra flag.

675
00:36:56,341 --> 00:36:58,300
Maybe there's a plaque next to the door.

676
00:36:58,301 --> 00:37:01,240
But otherwise, it's generic American office building.

677
00:37:01,241 --> 00:37:03,140
The Pentagon is not that.

678
00:37:03,141 --> 00:37:09,220
The Pentagon says, this represents us, the country.

679
00:37:09,221 --> 00:37:11,820
While the Pentagon's exterior became a landmark

680
00:37:11,821 --> 00:37:14,260
worthy of preservation, its interior

681
00:37:14,261 --> 00:37:17,120
became a hazard deserving of demolition.

682
00:37:17,121 --> 00:37:20,940
Unreliable infrastructure, outdated utility systems,

683
00:37:20,941 --> 00:37:24,040
and toxic materials such as asbestos, mercury,

684
00:37:24,140 --> 00:37:27,140
and lead paint plague the building that's even become

685
00:37:27,240 --> 00:37:30,180
infested with rats.

686
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:32,880
I personally experienced some of that in my office.

687
00:37:32,980 --> 00:37:35,720
I had a very large plant in my office

688
00:37:35,820 --> 00:37:37,560
and a very large tub.

689
00:37:37,660 --> 00:37:41,420
And there was a rat that would come in every week or so

690
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:45,900
and burrow away in it, throw the soil out on the floor,

691
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:49,560
on the carpet, and get into something or other.

692
00:37:50,940 --> 00:37:55,940
In 1993, the Pentagon observed its 50th anniversary.

693
00:37:56,040 --> 00:37:57,940
If the building was to function properly

694
00:37:58,040 --> 00:38:00,940
for at least another 50, experts determined

695
00:38:01,040 --> 00:38:03,980
that it would have to undergo a major overhaul

696
00:38:04,080 --> 00:38:06,780
to comply with current safety codes.

697
00:38:06,880 --> 00:38:09,800
Due to the Pentagon's landmark status

698
00:38:09,900 --> 00:38:13,000
and logistical considerations, tearing it down

699
00:38:13,100 --> 00:38:16,140
and starting over was not an option.

700
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:19,540
A full-scale renovation presented the best way

701
00:38:19,541 --> 00:38:22,780
the best way to make the much needed improvements.

702
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:28,980
Congress allocated $1.2 billion to complete the 15-year project.

703
00:38:29,080 --> 00:38:30,480
It's a historic undertaking.

704
00:38:30,580 --> 00:38:32,480
It's a very unique opportunity.

705
00:38:32,580 --> 00:38:35,080
It's the largest reconstruction effort ever accomplished

706
00:38:35,180 --> 00:38:36,600
in the history of the United States.

707
00:38:36,700 --> 00:38:40,200
It's really a remarkable thing to do.

708
00:38:40,300 --> 00:38:43,840
Lee Evey oversees the unprecedented project.

709
00:38:43,940 --> 00:38:46,640
His motto is on cost, on schedule,

710
00:38:46,740 --> 00:38:49,140
and built for the next 50 years.

711
00:38:49,141 --> 00:38:52,640
A daunting challenge considering the renovation's scope

712
00:38:52,740 --> 00:38:55,480
and the Pentagon's purpose.

713
00:38:55,580 --> 00:38:59,220
We say that it's kind of like going into your family room

714
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:01,480
and going to a black and white television set

715
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:03,560
and converting that black and white television set

716
00:39:03,660 --> 00:39:06,060
to a color television set without ever turning the set

717
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:09,260
off, without ever missing a single second of programming.

718
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:11,660
We can't shut down the Department of Defense

719
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:16,200
for our convenience while we renovate the Pentagon.

720
00:39:16,300 --> 00:39:19,140
The renovation began in 1993.

721
00:39:19,141 --> 00:39:22,040
with the construction of a $60 million

722
00:39:22,140 --> 00:39:25,640
state-of-the-art heating and refrigeration plant,

723
00:39:25,740 --> 00:39:28,380
the new low-emission boilers and chillers

724
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:34,680
generate enough capacity to heat and cool 12,000 homes.

725
00:39:34,780 --> 00:39:38,300
Since the plant became operational in 1997,

726
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:42,060
it has saved more than $6 million in reduced staff

727
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:44,800
and utility costs.

728
00:39:44,900 --> 00:39:48,440
This facility, along with experimental solar panels,

729
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:50,940
are part of the greening of the Pentagon,

730
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:53,740
a program the renovation team has initiated

731
00:39:53,840 --> 00:39:56,440
to make the building more energy efficient.

732
00:39:56,540 --> 00:39:59,420
The completion of the heating and refrigeration plant

733
00:39:59,520 --> 00:40:02,060
made way for the next and most dramatic phase

734
00:40:02,160 --> 00:40:05,360
of the renovation, the complete overhaul

735
00:40:05,460 --> 00:40:09,200
of the Pentagon itself, one wedge at a time,

736
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:12,360
a sequencing consistent with its original design

737
00:40:12,460 --> 00:40:14,260
and construction.

738
00:40:14,360 --> 00:40:16,140
The way that the Pentagon was originally designed

739
00:40:16,141 --> 00:40:18,440
and constructed has an impact on us.

740
00:40:18,441 --> 00:40:22,080
They actually built five chevron-shaped structures.

741
00:40:22,081 --> 00:40:24,680
Each one operates almost as an independent building

742
00:40:24,681 --> 00:40:26,080
separate from the others.

743
00:40:26,081 --> 00:40:29,280
Since they're built almost as individual standalone buildings,

744
00:40:29,281 --> 00:40:31,280
it enables us now in the renovation

745
00:40:31,281 --> 00:40:34,500
to segment or separate each one of those chevrons,

746
00:40:34,501 --> 00:40:36,600
move all the people out of that chevron,

747
00:40:36,601 --> 00:40:38,400
about 5,000 people,

748
00:40:38,401 --> 00:40:41,440
and then shut everything down inside that area

749
00:40:41,441 --> 00:40:43,340
without affecting the rest of the building.

750
00:40:46,140 --> 00:40:50,000
Work on Wedge One commenced in January 1998,

751
00:40:50,001 --> 00:40:53,340
when the renovation team began demolishing the interior

752
00:40:53,341 --> 00:40:56,380
of the 1 million square foot space.

753
00:40:56,381 --> 00:40:59,380
It removed 15 million pounds of debris,

754
00:40:59,381 --> 00:41:03,300
including 2,000 tons of hazardous asbestos,

755
00:41:03,301 --> 00:41:06,300
stripping the building down to its essential structure

756
00:41:06,301 --> 00:41:09,740
as at times resembled an archaeological dig,

757
00:41:09,741 --> 00:41:12,140
as the renovation team has unearthed relics

758
00:41:12,141 --> 00:41:15,140
from the building's original construction.

759
00:41:15,141 --> 00:41:16,640
The things that I find most interesting

760
00:41:16,740 --> 00:41:20,580
are the human kinds of touches, like a chalked, hand-drawn

761
00:41:20,680 --> 00:41:22,880
American flag up on one of the beams of the building

762
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:25,080
where someone went to great trouble

763
00:41:25,180 --> 00:41:28,780
to draw a 48-star American flag.

764
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:31,220
We've uncovered some old maps in various places

765
00:41:31,320 --> 00:41:33,120
in the building, showing a lot of countries that don't even

766
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:34,520
exist anymore.

767
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:36,800
Probably the most unusual thing that we've

768
00:41:36,900 --> 00:41:39,860
found was the engine block of this truck that apparently

769
00:41:39,960 --> 00:41:44,140
had mired in the soil and had just been built around

770
00:41:44,141 --> 00:41:46,580
and had been sitting there for 58 years.

771
00:41:49,280 --> 00:41:51,280
Once they cleared the wedge,

772
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:55,120
contractors set about completely rebuilding the interior

773
00:41:55,121 --> 00:41:57,880
and bringing it into the 21st century.

774
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:01,900
Each wedge will take about three years to turn around.

775
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:05,420
The treatment of the windows presents in microcosm

776
00:42:05,421 --> 00:42:07,600
the breadth and depth of the project.

777
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:11,360
Eventually, the renovation team will remove

778
00:42:11,361 --> 00:42:16,360
of all of the Pentagon's 7,748 windows,

779
00:42:16,361 --> 00:42:19,400
enough glass to cover seven acres,

780
00:42:19,401 --> 00:42:21,600
and replace them with bomb-resistant,

781
00:42:21,601 --> 00:42:24,940
double-pane thermal-insulated units.

782
00:42:24,941 --> 00:42:27,180
The building's landmark designation

783
00:42:27,181 --> 00:42:31,480
mandates that the windows resemble the 1942 originals,

784
00:42:31,481 --> 00:42:34,240
so decorative but non-functioning handles

785
00:42:34,241 --> 00:42:36,520
have been added to maintain the Pentagon's

786
00:42:36,521 --> 00:42:40,120
historic appearance.

787
00:42:40,121 --> 00:42:42,960
Preparing for the future while respecting the past

788
00:42:42,961 --> 00:42:47,440
demonstrates the passion the renovation team brings to the job.

789
00:42:48,200 --> 00:42:51,620
It's very rare to find it, and that's the level of commitment you have in this program.

790
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:55,380
It's the type of people who love to come here to work to do this thing,

791
00:42:55,720 --> 00:42:57,600
to leave a lasting contribution to their country.

792
00:42:59,280 --> 00:43:02,380
When the renovation is completed in 2015,

793
00:43:03,080 --> 00:43:07,000
the Pentagon once again will amaze all those who see it.

794
00:43:07,001 --> 00:43:10,680
Yet despite all the changes the building will have undergone,

795
00:43:11,160 --> 00:43:15,700
the Pentagon's most impressive feature will remain what it's always been,

796
00:43:16,120 --> 00:43:17,900
the people who work there.

797
00:43:18,540 --> 00:43:23,860
For more than 50 years, they've proven that the Pentagon is not a stagnant bureaucracy,

798
00:43:24,320 --> 00:43:26,820
but rather a vital community.

799
00:43:27,760 --> 00:43:30,080
There's a great cooperative spirit there.

800
00:43:30,260 --> 00:43:32,620
The feeling is it's such a tough bureaucracy.

801
00:43:32,800 --> 00:43:34,280
It's hard to get things done.

802
00:43:34,281 --> 00:43:39,120
And the only way you're going to survive and accomplish anything is to work together in

803
00:43:39,121 --> 00:43:40,460
a cooperative kind of way.

804
00:43:40,461 --> 00:43:42,500
And so that community is there.

805
00:43:42,501 --> 00:43:45,560
And people who worked in the Pentagon know that.

806
00:43:45,561 --> 00:43:48,400
And they're special kinds of people.

807
00:43:48,401 --> 00:43:53,920
You realize that you're working in a building that is a myth.

808
00:43:53,921 --> 00:43:55,720
And you're kind of proud of that.

809
00:43:55,721 --> 00:43:57,060
You're proud to be part of it.

810
00:43:57,061 --> 00:44:01,320
Because then you can tell your own stories about the Pentagon and some of the interesting

811
00:44:01,321 --> 00:44:04,440
things that happened to you because at the time they were very, very big.

812
00:44:04,441 --> 00:44:11,080
It was a period of history and in some way you were associated with it.

813
00:44:11,081 --> 00:44:15,700
It's a memorable experience to have had the opportunity to work here.

814
00:44:15,701 --> 00:44:20,080
And I think that deep down inside there's a great deal of pride on every individual that

815
00:44:20,081 --> 00:44:23,040
has worked in the Pentagon.

816
00:44:23,041 --> 00:44:24,680
They'll take that with them for the rest of their lives.

817
00:45:30,100 --> 00:45:31,300
I'm out.

